  "salt": "7EUgYBLQtQjZIIcJMYCBYQ==",
  "text": "Interpretatio Romana is a cosmological concept. It means Roman interpretation. When the Roman legions reached Celtic lands they met many gods. The Celts did not keep a tightly organized pantheon. They tied divinities to places, not to abstract roles. The Romans set out to make order from this. A healing goddess became Minerva. A powerful god became Mars. A god of commerce became Mercury. The old name was sometimes joined to the new. Sulis became Sulis Minerva in the town of Bath. In other places the old name was lost. The Romans built temples and carved statues, as to the horse goddess Epona. The Celts had not built or carved before then. Ireland never saw the Romans. It kept no tradition of renaming its gods. The Christian church later took the same path. It gave old Celtic gods the rank of saint. Many Celtic saints, notably Brigit, have pagan origins.",
  "objective": "Document the cosmological concept Interpretatio Romana.",
